Think of a Kafka Topic as a multi-lane highway, Partitions as the individual lanes that let traffic move in parallel, and Offsets as the permanent
mile-markers painted on each lane so every driver knows exactly how far they've traveled.

Technical Breakdown :-
These three primitives form the entire storage and scaling engine of Apache Kafka:

Topic: A logical category or feed name where producers publish events (e.g., user-clicks or payment-transactions).
It is purely a logical abstraction—similar to a table name in a database.

Partition: The physical unit of parallelism and storage. A Topic is split into one or more Partitions distributed across different Brokers in the cluster. 
Each Partition is an immutable, append-only log file. Ordering is strictly guaranteed within a single Partition, but never globally across the whole Topic.

Offset: A unique, monotonically increasing integer (0, 1, 2, 3...) assigned to every message as it is appended to a specific Partition. 
Crucially, Kafka does not delete messages once they are read; instead, each Consumer Group commits its own Offset
back to Kafka (in an internal __consumer_offsets topic) to bookmark its exact reading progress.

Buisness Context :-
This three-part design is what makes real-time enterprise pipelines both infinitely scalable and bulletproof. 
Because Offsets are tracked per Consumer Group, your Fraud Detection service can read the payments topic in real time at
Offset 10,000, while your Batch Analytics job wakes up once an hour and calmly catches up from Offset 4,000—using the exact 
same underlying data with zero duplication and zero risk of losing events during an outage.

Design Decision :- 
Some engineers often focus only on the happy path and leave enable.auto.commit=true on their consumers. 
If the consumer reads a batch, auto-commits the Offset, and then crashes before writing to the database, that data is silently lost forever.
As a data engineer, I disable auto-commit and explicitly commit Offsets only after the downstream write succeeds—guaranteeing at-least-once delivery. 
Furthermore, I treat Offset management as a superpower for disaster recovery: if a bug corrupts our downstream warehouse,
I don't beg upstream teams for a backfill;
I simply reset the Consumer Group's Offset to yesterday's timestamp and replay the exact historical stream.
